<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asia (Roman province)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/133-bce">133 BCE</a>–7th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asia was a Roman province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, formed from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> bequeathed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE. Organised by 129 BCE, it was among the richest provinces of the empire, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, its leading city and the governor's seat, Smyrna and Pergamon. It was an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, home of the Seven Churches of the Book of Revelation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province.jpg" alt="Asia (Roman province)" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Asia_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
